Your answer, upgraded to L5 level

    "The brute-force approach is to iterate over every element and scan to its right 
        until I find the first strictly greater element. In the worst case, this takes O(n²) 
        time and O(1) auxiliary space, excluding the output.

To optimize it, I use a monotonic decreasing stack of indices. 
The stack contains elements that have not yet found their next greater element. 
When I encounter the current element and it is greater than the element at the top of the stack, 
the current element becomes the first greater element to the right for that stack element, 
so I pop it and record the current value as its answer. I continue this while 
the current value is greater than the stack top, then push the current index.

The stack invariant is that the corresponding values are maintained in 
decreasing order, and every index in the stack represents an unresolved element.

Although there is a while loop inside the for loop, each index is pushed at 
most once and popped at most once. Therefore, there are at most O(n) push 
and O(n) pop operations overall, giving O(n) amortized time complexity. 
The auxiliary space is O(n)."

